nal distention, blood in stool, chronic constipation\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nImpacted Stool in Chronic Constipation vs Norovirus Infection:\n  Favours first:  bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, infrequent bowel movements, irritability\n  Favours second: diarrhea, fever, malaise, muscle pain, vomiting\n\nImpacted Stool in Chronic Constipation vs Ischemic Colitis:\n  Favours first:  bloating, feeling of incomplete evacuation, hard stools, headache, infrequent bowel movements\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abdominal tenderness, abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology\n\nNorovirus Infection vs Ischemic Colitis:\n  Favours first:  fever, headache, malaise, muscle pain\n  Favours second: abdominal swelling, abdominal tenderness, abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology\n\nRelated to Ischemic Colitis: colitis (disease), vascular disease\n\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Pathophysiologically, chronic constipation generally results from inadequate fiber or fluid intake or from disordered colonic transit or anorectal function. These result from neurogastroenterologic di\n[RAG context for 'A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with nausea and vomiting for the past week' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Altered Bowel Habits Altered bowel habits are common complaints of patients with GI disease. Constipation is reported as infrequent defecation, straining with defecation, passage of hard stools, or a \n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.66):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Pathophysiologically, chronic constipation generally results from inadequate fiber or fluid intake or from disordered colonic transit or anorectal function. These result from neurogastroenterologic di\n[RAG context for 'He decided to come in when his symptoms worsened' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Altered Bowel Habits Altered bowel habits are common complaints of patients with GI disease. Constipation is reported as infrequent defecation, straining with defecation, passage of hard stools, or a \n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  nausea and vomiting exacerbated by large fatty meals and alcohol → gastroparesis → Functional / Motility (Neurogenic) GI Disorder\n    (frequency: occasional)\n    Suggestion: Investigate for diabetic gastroparesis via gastric emptying study to support the diagnosis of neurogenic GI motility disorder\n  chronic constipation with nausea → fecal impaction → Impacted Stool in Chronic Constipation\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Perform abdominal X-ray or digital rectal exam to confirm fecal impaction as the underlying mechanism"}
